WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.294
Security in ASP.

2
00:00:01.294 --> 00:00:06.468
NET Core is not one switch, one middleware call, or one final release checklist.

3
00:00:06.468 --> 00:00:11.578
It is a set of engineering decisions made throughout the application lifecycle.

4
00:00:11.578 --> 00:00:15.005
Architecture defines trust boundaries and data flows.

5
00:00:15.005 --> 00:00:19.145
Coding choices define how requests are validated and authorized.

6
00:00:19.145 --> 00:00:25.031
Configuration controls identity providers, secrets, cookies, diagnostics, and environments.

7
00:00:25.031 --> 00:00:33.698
Deployment and operations determine whether the application remains secure when real users, real data, and real failures are involved.

8
00:00:33.698 --> 00:00:34.991
ASP.

9
00:00:34.991 --> 00:00:41.912
NET Core gives teams a strong foundation, but the framework does not know the application's business rules.

10
00:00:41.912 --> 00:00:54.654
It cannot decide which endpoints require authentication, which roles are too broad, which tenant owns a resource, which fields should appear in a response, or which events should generate an alert.

11
00:00:54.654 --> 00:01:01.962
Framework defaults help reduce common mistakes, but they work best when paired with deliberate design and review.

12
00:01:01.962 --> 00:01:11.988
The request pipeline, identity model, authorization policies, Data Protection configuration, error handling, logging, and deployment settings all interact.

13
00:01:11.988 --> 00:01:15.998
A secure application depends on those pieces working together.

14
00:01:15.998 --> 00:01:20.590
A strong authentication setup does not help if endpoints are left open.

15
00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:24.794
Careful validation does not help if sensitive details are logged.

16
00:01:24.794 --> 00:01:30.486
Secure cookies do not help if session behavior and logout expectations are not reviewed.

17
00:01:30.486 --> 00:01:34.366
Threat modeling does not need to be ceremonial to be useful.

18
00:01:34.366 --> 00:01:50.795
Teams can ask simple questions during design and review: who is calling this endpoint, what data crosses the boundary, what is trusted, what must be checked on the server, what can fail, what should be logged, and what sensitive data must not be exposed.

19
00:01:50.795 --> 00:01:56.228
These questions turn security from an afterthought into normal engineering practice.

20
00:01:56.228 --> 00:02:01.467
This course focuses on defensive fundamentals for professional application teams.

21
00:02:01.467 --> 00:02:18.348
The goal is to build a practical baseline: explicit authentication, server-side authorization, safe state handling, input validation, output encoding, careful configuration, protected secrets, useful logging, and production behavior that can be operated safely.
